#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177087 :tutorial/content "We know how to find $2^{-1}$. But what about $2^{-2}, 2^{-3},2^{-4},\\ldots$?\n\nTo work out ${\\color{blue}{2}}^{{\\color{red}{-2}}}$, all we need to do is find the reciprocal of the base $({\\color{blue}{2}})$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $({{\\color{red}{+2}}})$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{-2} &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{2}) \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1 \\cdot 1}{2 \\cdot 2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA similar approach can be used for other powers. Let's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "d6f8e85f-3130-5d56-9648-0225b32a7478" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8025 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
